3"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "780e7464-55bc-348e-96a4-9d7103a5d81a"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96d-ff6c-7094-a6dd-512c50fb3e84"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96e-0012-7d66-a558-3c38f1b0ec11"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96d-ff6c-7094-a6dd-512c50fb3e85"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96e-00c8-7fa2-9c66-c33df390079e"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96e-0012-7d66-a558-3c38f1b0ec12"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "9a4dd4ef-f938-3930-9ca3-ff81515b8b03"}}, :outputs #{#propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "08069eba-ec05-3c65-9bb8-0b325256056e"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "780e7464-55bc-348e-96a4-9d7103a5d81a"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96d-ff6c-7094-a6dd-512c50fb3e85"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96e-00c8-7fa2-9c66-c33df390079d"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96e-00c8-7fa2-9c66-c33df39007ae"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96e-0012-7d66-a558-3c38f1b0ec10"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96d-ff6c-7094-a6dd-512c50fb3e83"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a96e-0012-7d66-a558-3c38f1b0ec12"} #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "9a4dd4ef-f938-3930-9ca3-ff81515b8b03"}}}, model-result {:edge #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "1747a5d2-373b-3b71-a57b-cb1341b75ad5"}, :value {:kind :reply, :text "\n\n(define conv-source (behavior:source))\n(define conv-history (behavior:scan conv-source seq:append (seq:empty)))\n(define conv-latest (behavior:latest-value conv-history (seq:empty)))\n\n(define conv-reply (network [msg-record]\n  (let [ack (message-to-user msg-record)]\n    (-> (event:point (data:get :id ack) (data:get :time ack) (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :content ack))) conv-source)\n    ack)))\n\n(-> (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) (data:record :role \"user\" :content (data:get :content user-ingress))) conv-source)\n\n(define conv-request (network [user-record]\n  (let [prior (behavior:latest-value conv-history (seq:empty))\n        sys-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"To reply, emit executable Lain: (conv-reply (data:record :id \\\"assistant-N\\\" :time T :role \\\"assistant\\\" :content \\\"your reply text\\\")). T must be user time + 1. N must differ from the user id. Return only that form.\")\n        msgs (seq:append (seq:append (seq:empty) sys-msg) (seq:append (seq:empty) (data:record :role \"user\" :content (data:get :content user-record))))\n        request (data:record :id (data:get :id user-record) :messages msgs)]\n    (model:request request))))\n\n(-> (cell:value (cell:reference conv-latest)) (cell:input (data:get :feedback-value memory-cell)))\n(-> (cell:reference conv-latest) (cell:input (data:get :feedback-edge memory-cell)))", :thinking "Let me analyze this complex task carefully. I need to construct a live conversation network in Lain (a connected dataflow language, not Clojure).\n\nKey requirements:\n1. Build history with temporal reducers (behavior:scan, behavior:map, behavior:event-filter)\n2. Preserve chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies\n3. Connect user-ingress to model requests\n4. Define a reusable reply function that delivers to user, then adds acknowledged reply to history\n5. Subsequent requests must contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages\n6. Each subsequent model response must be executable Lain calling the reply function with a new assistant message\n7. Conversation requests include an explicit system message containing the reply-function calling convention\n8. Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation history\n9. Use the same model throughout\n10. Use a different name for the conversation accumulator\n11. memory-cell is a reference-valued plain record - read fields with data:get, use cell:input on memory references\n12. Do not send a request until a user event arrives\n13. Unknown inputs suspend computation; avoid cycles requiring a future reply before first request\n14. model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target\n15. Inspect diagnostics if construction fails\n\nKey constraints from the language introduction:\n- Lain is NOT Clojure\n- Return raw parenthesized forms only; no triple-backtick fences\n- A callable is invoked as (f x); (-> x f) writes to the callable cell and does NOT call f\n- Only binding/argument vectors use literal brackets; construct data vectors with seq:empty and seq:append\n- Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects\n- A scan takes its source as its first argument; do not write events to the scan result\n- behavior:scan, map, and event-filter produce behavior objects, not ordinary records or vectors\n- Use behavior:latest-value to project a selected behavior value before passing to ordinary primitives\n- An optional fallback makes an empty behavior usable without inventing an occurrence\n- seq:project projects one key, so (seq:project :role xs) returns role strings, not message records\n- Model messages must be records with string :role and :content\n- System instructions must be first\n- The user ingress is a message RECORD, not a behavior or callable\n- Must capture its occurrences with event:point if you need a temporal source\n- (define name expression) binds a cell\n- (define pending) declares an unknown cell\n- (network [x y] expression) defines a callable\n- (let [x expression y expression] body) binds locals\n- (-> value target-cell) connects cells\n- (if condition yes no) selects a branch\n- Qualified names avoid editor collisions\n- Use data:record for immutable plain records\n- (data:record :key value) constructs a record; (data:get :key record) reads it\n- seq:empty [] returns an empty vector; seq:append [vector element] appends one element\n- seq:concat [vector ...] concatenates vectors into one flat vector\n- seq:last [vector] reads the last element\n- seq:project [key vector] projects a key over a vector\n- data:equal? [a b] compares values\n- event:point [id numeric-time value] captures a retained temporal occurrence\n- Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences\n- text:format [template & values] formats %s placeholders\n- behavior:source [] creates a reducer cell; connect supported event:point results to it with ->\n- behavior:scan [source reducer initial] emits a state at EVERY ordered occurrence\n- behavior:event-filter [behavior predicate] keeps matching historical occurrences\n- behavior:map [behavior callable] maps values\n- behavior:latest-value [behavior optional-fallback] projects its most recent value\n- model:request [record] submits { :id string :messages vector-of-role-content-records }\n- user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content\n- Assistant time should be user time + 1 and assistant id must differ from user id\n- message-to-user [record] acknowledges a record containing :id :time :role assistant :content\n- source:admit [complete-reply lexical-target support-cell-reference] admits complete forms sequentially\n- environment:overview [environment optional-name-vector] lists visible binding titles\n- cell:reference [cell] yields an inspectable reference\n- inspect:cell [reference] exposes :edge, :value, :inputs, :outputs\n- cell:value [reference] connects that edge's strongest value directly to an ordinary cell\n- cell:input [reference] returns a writable port for the referenced edge\n- Memory record fields contain references, so write through cell:input, not into a slot read projection\n- data:snapshot [compound] resolves a finite compound into a plain record\n\nFrom the construction-review-cell:\n- Each call to behavior:source creates a DISTINCT source\n- Event identity and time must vary with actual events\n- A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temp